%[text] %[text:anchor:H_D5978926] ## Transfer function representation of a DC motor
%[text] Another essential electric vehicle (EV) component is the DC motor. 
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![engineFocus.png](text:image:5e9d)
%[text]{"align":"center"} *Simplified EV schematic*
%[text] The purpose of the DC motor is to convert electrical energy to mechanical. The mechanical energy is manifested as a torque on the drive shaft. This torque is converted to a traction force by the wheels, which causes the vehicle to move.
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![EVSimpleSystemMotorHighlight.png](text:image:9cb1)
%[text]{"align":"center"} *The DC motor bridges the electrical and physical domains.*
%[text] A DC motor works by conducting a current through an armature that can rotate freely. The fixed brushes transmit the current to the rotating commutators. The current flowing through the armature passes through a magnetic field generated by the stator magnets, resulting in a Lorentz force in the perpendicular direction ($\\mathbf{F} = i\\mathbf{\\ell} \\times \\mathbf{B}${"altText":"\mathbf{F} = i\mathbf{\ell} \times \mathbf{B}"}, where $\\mathbf{\\ell}${"altText":"\mathbf{\ell}"} points in the wire direction). The two sides of the armature each produce a force, resulting in a net torque on the rotor $T${"altText":"T"}. If sufficient torque is applied, the shaft rotates, changing the angular position $\\theta${"altText":"\theta"}.
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![motorDiagram.png](text:image:190a)
%[text]{"align":"center"} *DC motor schematic. A net torque* $T${"altText":"T"} *is generated on the armature by Lorentz forces. In the electrical domain, the motor is represented by a series of three circuit elements.*
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_71CBE6D7] **Derivation of the DC motor differential equations**
%[text] Under typical operating conditions, the torque generated by a DC motor is directly proportional to the current passing through it:
%[text]  $T = k\_mi${"altText":"T = k\_mi"}
%[text] where $k\_m${"altText":"k\_m"} is a proportionality constant determined by the characteristics of the specific motor. This relationship fails for large values of $i${"altText":"i"}. 
%[text] When the armature rotates, it generates a back electromotive force ([back EMF](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Counter-electromotive_force)) that opposes the applied voltage. In the circuit diagram, this is represented by a motor symbol. The voltage drop due to the back EMF, denoted $e${"altText":"e"}, is proportional to the rotational velocity of the armature:
%[text]  $e =k\_e \\dot{\\theta}${"altText":"e =k\_e \dot{\theta}"}
%[text] where $k\_e${"altText":"k\_e"} is a constant of proportionality. To derive the relationship between the current $i${"altText":"i"} and the motor shaft angular position $\\theta${"altText":"\theta"}, you can apply Kirchhoff's voltage law and Newton's second law in terms of torques acting on the motor shaft. Kirchhoff's voltage law implies that the sum of the voltage drops across the circuit must equal the applied voltage:
%[text]  $iR + L \\frac{di}{dt} + k\_e \\dot{\\theta} = v${"altText":"iR + L \frac{di}{dt} + k\_e \dot{\theta} = v"} $(1)${"altText":"(1)"}
%[text] where $v${"altText":"v"} is the electric potential applied to the motor. In the absence of other applied torques, the equation of motion can be derived by balancing the inertia of the motor shaft ($J \\ddot{\\theta}${"altText":"J \ddot{\theta}"}) with the applied torque ($T${"altText":"T"}) and frictional resistance ($B \\dot{\\theta}${"altText":"B \dot{\theta}"}).
%[text]  $J \\ddot{\\theta}  = T - B \\dot{\\theta}${"altText":"J \ddot{\theta}  = T - B \dot{\theta}"}
%[text] Using the linear relationship between torque and current, this is equivalent to:
%[text]  $J \\ddot{\\theta} + B \\dot{\\theta}  = k\_m i${"altText":"J \ddot{\theta} + B \dot{\theta}  = k\_m i"} $(2)${"altText":"(2)"}
%%
%[text] ![write.png](text:image:44ce) **Exercise.** In this exercise, you will compute transfer functions of the DC motor system. Assume that the system starts at rest ($i = i' = \\theta = \\dot{\\theta} = 0${"altText":"i = i' = \theta = \dot{\theta} = 0"}). Also, assume that the applied voltage is a function of time $v = v(t)${"altText":"v = v(t)"}.
%[text] **(a)** Compute the Laplace transform of the voltage conservation equation (1) and apply the initial conditions. Type your result below in terms of the defined symbolic variables. 
%[text] ![matlab.png](text:image:5e18) **Pro-tip**: When you write a symbolic equation, you assign the whole equation to a MATLAB variable using the assignment operator "$=${"altText":"="}" (single equals). The equals sign in the symbolic equation is written using "`=="` (double equals). For example: 
%[text] ```matlabCodeExample
%[text] syms y m x b
%[text] linearEq = y == m*x + b
%[text] ```
% Use these symbolic variables
syms L R k_e J B k_m    % Constants
syms Theta I s V        % Laplace domain variables
% Write your laplace transform equation here
eqn1 = R*I + L*s*I + s*k_e*Theta == V
checkEqn1(eqn1)
%[text] **(b)** Compute the Laplace transform of the equation of motion (2) and apply the initial conditions. Type your answer below in terms of the defined symbolic variables. 
% Write your laplace transform equation here
eqn2 = J*s^2*Theta + B*s*Theta == k_m*I
checkEqn2(eqn2)
%[text] **(c)** Solve for the angular displacement transfer function $G(s) = \\frac{ \\Theta(s) }{V(s)}${"altText":"G(s) = \frac{ \Theta(s) }{V(s)}"} using your results from **(a)** and **(b)**.
% Write your transfer function here
ThetaOverV = k_m/(s*(L*J*s^2 + (R*J+L*B)*s + B*R + k_m*k_e))
checkThetaOverV(ThetaOverV)
%[text] **(d)** Solve for the angular velocity transfer function $H(s) = \\frac{\\Omega(s)}{V(s)}${"altText":"H(s) = \frac{\Omega(s)}{V(s)}"} where $\\Omega${"altText":"\Omega"} is the Laplace transform of the angular velocity $\\omega(t) = \\dot{\\theta}(t)${"altText":"\omega(t) = \dot{\theta}(t)"}.
% Write your transfer function here
OmegaOverV = k_m/(L*J*s^2 + (R*J+L*B)*s + B*R + k_m*k_e)
checkOmegaOverV(OmegaOverV)
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_62C365FA] ## Pole analysis
%[text] You can gain insight into the characteristics of a dynamic system without actually solving it by analyzing the form of its transfer function. The poles of the transfer function offer the most insight.
%[text] **Definition.** The **poles** of a rational transfer function occur where the denominator is zero. Consider a rational transfer function in factored form:
%[text]  $G(s) = K \\frac{ (s-z\_1)(s-z\_2) \\ldots (s-z\_m) }{ (s-p\_1)(s-p\_2) \\ldots (s-p\_n)}${"altText":"G(s) = K \frac{ (s-z\_1)(s-z\_2) \ldots (s-z\_m) }{ (s-p\_1)(s-p\_2) \ldots (s-p\_n)}"}
%[text] The poles are $p\_1${"altText":"p\_1"}, $p\_2${"altText":"p\_2"}, $\\ldots${"altText":"\ldots"}, $p\_n${"altText":"p\_n"}.
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_2BF30749] ### Stability
%[text] The real parts of the poles determine the stability of the system.
%[text] 1. A pole with a positive real part ($\\text{Re}(p\_i) \> 0${"altText":"\text{Re}(p\_i) \> 0"}) characterizes an exponential growth component of the solution.
%[text] 2. A pole with a negative real part ($\\text{Re}(p\_i) \< 0${"altText":"\text{Re}(p\_i) \< 0"}) characterizes an exponential decay component of the solution.
%[text] 3. A pole with a zero real part ($\\text{Re}(p\_i) = 0${"altText":"\text{Re}(p\_i) = 0"}) characterizes a steady component of the solution.  \
%[text] Poles with an imaginary component ($\\text{Im}(p\_i) \\neq 0${"altText":"\text{Im}(p\_i) \neq 0"}) generate oscillatory behavior. Such poles necessarily come in conjugate pairs (so that their product is real). 
%[text]{"align":"center"}  ![polePlaneV4.png](text:image:58e9)
%[text]{"align":"center"}  *Impulse responses characterized by the position of poles in the complex* $s${"altText":"s"}*-plane. Poles in the left-half plane (where* $\\text{Re}(p) \< 0${"altText":"\text{Re}(p) \< 0"}*) are stable. The poles in the bottom-half plane are the conjugate poles required for the imaginary poles.*
%%
