%[text] %[text:anchor:H_D4FEC02F] ### Natural frequency
%[text] The locations of the poles in the $s${"altText":"s"}-plane determine the natural oscillation frequencies present in the system.
%[text] - The natural (undamped) frequency (in rad/s) contributed by a pole $p${"altText":"p"} is the pole magnitude, $r = |p|${"altText":"r = |p|"}.
%[text] - The phase, $\\phi = \\arg p${"altText":"\phi = \arg p"}, determines the relative strength of the oscillatory component. \
%[text]  ![complexPhaseMag.png](text:image:8a83)
%[text]  *Phase* $\\phi${"altText":"\phi"} *and magnitude* $r${"altText":"r"} *of pole* $p${"altText":"p"} *shown in the complex plane*
%%
%[text] ![interact.png](text:image:6daf) **Activity.** In this activity, you will explore how poles relate to the natural frequency response.
%[text] Consider the transfer function:
%[text]  $H(s) = \\frac{1}{s^2 -2\\,\\mathrm{cos}\\left(\\phi \\right)\\,r\\,s+r^2}${"altText":"H(s) = \frac{1}{s^2 -2,\mathrm{cos}\left(\phi \right),r,s+r^2}"},  which is constructed with poles: $p = r(\\cos \\phi \\pm i\\sin \\phi)${"altText":"p = r(\cos \phi \pm i\sin \phi)"}.
%[text] 1. The transfer function poles are plotted below. Change the slider values to $(r,\\phi) =${"altText":"(r,\phi) ="} $(1,135^\\circ)${"altText":"(1,135^\circ)"}. Are oscillations present in the impulse response?
%[text] 2. Set $(r,\\phi) =${"altText":"(r,\phi) ="} $(1,100^\\circ)${"altText":"(1,100^\circ)"} and then $(r,\\phi) =${"altText":"(r,\phi) ="} $(1,90^\\circ)${"altText":"(1,90^\circ)"}. Does the frequency of the oscillation change? Are the oscillations more prominent?
%[text] 3. Set $(r,\\phi) =${"altText":"(r,\phi) ="} $(1.5,90^\\circ)${"altText":"(1.5,90^\circ)"} and then $(r,\\phi) =${"altText":"(r,\phi) ="} $(2,90^\\circ)${"altText":"(2,90^\circ)"}. Does the frequency of the oscillation change? Are the oscillations more prominent? \
r = 1.75;        % Default: 1 %[control:slider:9ff3]{"position":[5,9]}
phiDeg = 110;     % Default: 45 %[control:slider:1ade]{"position":[10,13]}
plotPoles(r,phiDeg) % Plot the poles using a helper function 
