%[text] %[text:anchor:H_D280EDED] ### Analysis of a second-order system
%[text] Second-order systems with a pair of conjugate poles and no zeros are useful for illustrating the natural frequency response analytically. It is helpful to write this type of system in the form:
%[text]  $G(s) = \\frac{ K }{s^2 + 2 \\zeta \\omega\_n s  + \\omega\_n^2${"altText":"G(s) = \frac{ K }{s^2 + 2 \zeta \omega\_n s  + \omega\_n^2"}
%[text] where 
%[text] - $K${"altText":"K"} is the gain coefficient
%[text] - $\\zeta${"altText":"\zeta"} is the damping ratio
%[text] -  and $\\omega\_n${"altText":"\omega\_n"} is the (undamped) natural frequency.  \
%[text] These parameters are helpful for drawing a connection between the natural response of the system and the properties of the poles. In this section, the poles of the second-order system will be analytically computed and related to the natural response of the system.
%[text] **Pole analysis**
%[text] The poles of the system occur when the denominator is zero:
%[text]  $p = - \\zeta \\omega\_n \\pm \\omega\_n \\sqrt{\\zeta^2 - 1 }${"altText":"p = - \zeta \omega\_n \pm \omega\_n \sqrt{\zeta^2 - 1 }"}
%[text] For simplicity, consider $\\zeta \\in \[0,1)${"altText":"\zeta \in \[0,1)"}. In this case, the poles are
%[text]  $p = - \\zeta \\omega\_n \\pm i \\omega\_n \\sqrt{1 - \\zeta^2}${"altText":"p = - \zeta \omega\_n \pm i \omega\_n \sqrt{1 - \zeta^2}"}
%[text] As stated above, the natural frequency is defined as the magnitude of the pole. The magnitude is computed as $\\sqrt{ Re^2 + Im^2}${"altText":"\sqrt{ Re^2 + Im^2}"}:
%[text]  $\\begin{array}{rl} |p|\n &= \\sqrt{ (-\\zeta \\omega\_n)^2 + ( \\pm \\omega\_n \\sqrt{1-\\zeta^2})^2 } \\\\ \n &= \\sqrt{ \\zeta^2 \\omega\_n^2 + \\omega\_n^2 (1-\\zeta^2) } \\\\ \n &= |\\omega\_n|\\ \n\\end{array}${"altText":"\begin{array}{rl} |p|  &= \sqrt{ (-\zeta \omega\_n)^2 + ( \pm \omega\_n \sqrt{1-\zeta^2})^2 }    &= \sqrt{ \zeta^2 \omega\_n^2 + \omega\_n^2 (1-\zeta^2) }    &= |\omega\_n|  \end{array}"}
%[text] This illustrates why this form of the equation is used: the natural frequency is the parameter $\\omega\_n${"altText":"\omega\_n"}. 
%[text] The damping ratio $\\zeta${"altText":"\zeta"} determines the relative strength of the exponential part of the response. As $\\zeta \\rightarrow 1${"altText":"\zeta \rightarrow 1"}, the complex part of the pole tends to zero, implying less oscillatory and stronger exponential behavior. For stable systems, that implies greater damping. The gain coefficient $K${"altText":"K"} affects the magnitude of the response, not the time-dependent behavior.
%[text] **Natural response**
%[text] The impulse response is also called the natural response. The natural response $x(t)${"altText":"x(t)"} is computed by taking the inverse Laplace transform of
%[text]  $\\frac{X(s)}{U(s)} = \\frac{X(s)}{1} = \\frac{ K }{s^2 + 2 \\zeta \\omega\_n s  + \\omega\_n^2${"altText":"\frac{X(s)}{U(s)} = \frac{X(s)}{1} = \frac{ K }{s^2 + 2 \zeta \omega\_n s  + \omega\_n^2"}
%[text] because the impulse $u(t) = \\delta(t)${"altText":"u(t) = \delta(t)"} has a Laplace transform $U(s) = 1${"altText":"U(s) = 1"}. You can find the response's analytic form by referring to a table or using the [`ilaplace`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/symbolic/sym.ilaplace.html) function.
syms s zeta omega_n K X(s) x(t)
X(s) = K/(s^2 + 2*zeta*omega_n*s + omega_n^2) 
x(t) = ilaplace(X) % The impulse response in the time domain
%[text] Notice that the impulse response is a product of an exponential and sine function. From these functions observe that:
%[text] - The sine function frequency (in rads/s) is $\\omega\_n \\sqrt{1-\\zeta^2}${"altText":"\omega\_n \sqrt{1-\zeta^2}"}. If the damping $\\zeta = 0${"altText":"\zeta = 0"}, then the frequency is $\\omega\_n${"altText":"\omega\_n"}. This is the reason $\\omega\_n${"altText":"\omega\_n"} is often referred to as the *undamped* natural frequency.
%[text] - The rate of decay of the exponential damping function is $\\omega\_n \\zeta${"altText":"\omega\_n \zeta"}.
%[text] - The gain parameter $K${"altText":"K"} only contributes as a constant multiplier to the magnitude of the response. \
%%
%[text] ![interact.png](text:image:627a) **Activity.** Adjust the sliders to view different time-domain responses for the classical second-order system:
%[text]  $G(s) = \\frac{ K }{s^2 + 2 \\zeta \\omega\_n s  + \\omega\_n^2${"altText":"G(s) = \frac{ K }{s^2 + 2 \zeta \omega\_n s  + \omega\_n^2"}
K = 5; %[control:slider:9abb]{"position":[5,6]}
zeta = 0.2; %[control:slider:7133]{"position":[8,11]}
omega_n = 2; %[control:slider:15e9]{"position":[11,12]}

plotNaturalResponse(zeta,omega_n,K) % Helper function that plots the response for the second order system
