%[text] %[text:anchor:H_254B2217] ![write.png](text:image:2365) **Exercise.**  Mass-spring-damper analysis
%[text] You can analyze a mass-spring-damper by looking at the poles of its transfer function.
%[text]  ![mass\_spring\_damper2-flipped.png](text:image:7c49)
%[text]  *Mass-spring-damper diagram*
%[text] Consider a mass-spring-damper that
%[text] - is dynamically forced by an arbitrary function $u(t)${"altText":"u(t)"}
%[text] - has zero initial conditions: $x(0) = 0${"altText":"x(0) = 0"} and $x'(0)=0${"altText":"x'(0)=0"} \
%[text] The position transfer function is
%[text]  $G(s) = \\frac{X(s)}{U(s)} = \\frac{1}{ m s^2 + cs + k}${"altText":"G(s) = \frac{X(s)}{U(s)} = \frac{1}{ m s^2 + cs + k}"}
%[text] **(a)** Write the mass-spring-damper transfer function in the form 
%[text]  $G(s) = \\frac{ K }{s^2 + 2 \\zeta \\omega\_n s  + \\omega\_n^2${"altText":"G(s) = \frac{ K }{s^2 + 2 \zeta \omega\_n s  + \omega\_n^2"}
%[text] and solve for the expressions of the gain $K${"altText":"K"}, damping ratio $\\zeta${"altText":"\zeta"}, and natural frequency $\\omega\_n${"altText":"\omega\_n"} in terms of the mass-spring-damper parameters. Write your answers using the symbolic variables $c${"altText":"c"}, $m${"altText":"m"}, and $k${"altText":"k"}.
% Use these symbolic variables
syms m c k
% Replace the NaNs with your expressions
K = 1/m
omega_n = sqrt(k/m)
zeta = c/(2*sqrt(m*k))

checkMSDExpressions(K,omega_n,zeta) % Checks your answers
%%
%[text] **(b)** Solve for the symbolic expressions of the poles of $G${"altText":"G"} in terms of the mass-spring-damper parameters $m${"altText":"m"}, $c${"altText":"c"}, and $k${"altText":"k"}. Store the expressions below in `pplus` and `pminus` where `pplus` stores the positive root.
% Write your answers here
poles = solve(m*s^2+c*s+k == 0);
pplus = poles(1)
pminus = poles(2)
checkMSDPoles(pplus,pminus) % Checks the answers
%%
%[text] **(c)** You can gain insight into the dynamics of the system by examining how the poles change in the $s${"altText":"s"}-plane when parameters are varied. 
%[text] - The graph below shows the pole locations under variations of a parameter starting with $k = m = 1${"altText":"k = m = 1"} and $c = 0${"altText":"c = 0"}.
%[text] - Adjust the dropdown to change which parameter is varied.
%[text] - The curves plot the pole locations between the specified parameter's starting value and maximum value. Try changing the dropdown and the `maxValue` to observe changes to the poles.
%[text] - Use the plot to answer the reflection questions. \
variation = "c"; %[control:dropdown:961c]{"position":[13,16]}
maxValue = 3; %[control:slider:0072]{"position":[12,13]}

plotPoleVariation(variation,maxValue) % Helper function to generate the plot
