%[text] %[text:anchor:H_3EC17860] ## Zero analysis
%[text] The analysis of zeros is less straight-forward than the analysis of the poles of the system, because their effects are less pronounced. Zeros contribute to
%[text] - the transient behavior of the response,
%[text] - the magnitude of the response, and
%[text] - the response to different input frequencies. \
%[text] ![interact.png](text:image:34f5) **Activity.** Consider the simple system:
%[text]  $G(s) = \\frac{ s-z\_0}{s+1}${"altText":"G(s) = \frac{ s-z\_0}{s+1}"}
%[text] Adjust the location of the zero using the slider and observe the changes to the response.
z0 = 1.5; % Position of the zero %[control:slider:1633]{"position":[6,9]}
G = tf([1 -z0],[1 1]);           % The transfer function G(s)
axlim1 = [-3 3 -3 3];            % Axis limits for the first graph
axlim2 = [0 10 -5 5];            % Axis limits for the second graph
pzmapAndImpulse(G,axlim1,axlim2) % Generates the plot
%[text] ![brightbulb.png](text:image:922b) **Reflect.** 
%[text] - Is the stability or long-term behavior of the system affected by the zero location?
%[text] - How is the transient response affected by the zero?
%[text] - Does the system become unstable when the zero is in the right-half plane?
%[text] - What happens as the zero approaches the pole? \
%%
%[text] ![interact.png](text:image:9a41) **Activity.** In this activity, you will visually explore the relationship between transfer function zeros and time-domain responses. You will inspect the step responses of
%[text]  $G = \\frac{s-z\_0}{s^2 + 2s + 2}${"altText":"G = \frac{s-z\_0}{s^2 + 2s + 2}"}
%[text] - Begin the activity by running the attached app, [polesApp.mlapp](file:..\\..\\Apps\\polesApp.mlapp). Note that this link will only work if `polesApp.mlapp` is in the current folder or on the MATLAB Search Path.
%[text] - Set the number of poles/pole pairs to 1
%[text] - Place the pole pair at $p = -1 \\pm i${"altText":"p = -1 \pm i"}.
%[text] - View the **step** response by selecting *Step* in the *Response type* menu.
%[text] - Add a zero to the plot. Adjust the position of the zero to generate the step responses shown below. \
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![zeroPlotPart1.png](text:image:563c)
showAnswers = false; %[control:checkbox:5716]{"position":[15,20]}
showZeroPart1Answers(showAnswers)
%[text] ![brightbulb.png](text:image:212c) **Reflect.** 
%[text] - The transient of the step response that exceeds the steady value ($y = 1${"altText":"y = 1"}, in this case) is known as the **overshoot**. As the zero moves further in the negative direction along the real axis, what happens to the overshoot?
%[text] - The time it takes to reach the steady value ($y = 1${"altText":"y = 1"}, in this case) is known as the **rise time**. How is the rise time affected by changes to the zero, $z\_0${"altText":"z\_0"}?
%[text] - Try other values of the poles and zero, do you observe similar changes to the overshoot and rise time? \
%%
%[text] ![write.png](text:image:1a3b) **Exercise.**  In this exercise, you will examine the changes to the analytic step response of a transfer function with a single zero.
%[text] Consider the transfer function used in the previous activity:
%[text]  $G = \\frac{s-z\_0}{s^2 + 2s + 2}${"altText":"G = \frac{s-z\_0}{s^2 + 2s + 2}"}
%[text] **(a)** Compute the symbolic form of the **step** response of the transfer function, $x(t)${"altText":"x(t)"}. You can perform the analytic computations by hand or using the Symbolic Math Toolbox. 
% Use these symbolic variables
syms s z_0 t positive 
% Write your solution here
G = (s - z_0)/(s^2 + 2*s + 2);
U = 1/s;
GU = G*U;
x = ilaplace(GU)
%%
%[text] **(b)** Plot the analytic step response with $z\_0 = -1${"altText":"z\_0 = -1"} on the interval $t \\in \[0, 10\]${"altText":"t \in \[0, 10\]"}. If you computed the step response function with the Symbolic Math Toolbox, you can use the [`matlabFunction`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/symbolic/sym.matlabfunction.html) command to convert it to a function handle that you can evaluate.
% Create your plot here
figure
z0 = -1;
xf = matlabFunction(x);
tvec = linspace(0,10);
plot(tvec,xf(tvec,z0));
xlabel("t")
ylabel("x")
%%
%[text] **(c)** Using the analytic form of the transfer function, compute the limit of the function as $t \\to \\infty${"altText":"t \to \infty"}.
tlim = -z_0/2
%%
%[text] ![brightbulb.png](text:image:28d8) **Reflect.** 
%[text] - Vary the location of the zero ($z\_0${"altText":"z\_0"}) and observe how the step response changes.
%[text] - How does the location of the zero ($z\_0${"altText":"z\_0"}) affect the overall magnitude of the step response? Where does this show up in the analytic solution?
%[text] - How does the location of the zero ($z\_0${"altText":"z\_0"}) affect the rise time and overshoot? Where does this show up in the analytic solution? \
